## APPLICATION CODEC OPTIONS

Application codecs are protocol-aware shims, not raw TCP forwards. The server
must allow a codec globally and the client key must list it in
**auth_keys.meta**.

@options
@opt **--codec-allow** *name*
@cli values: monero-rpc
@cli spell: --codec-allow <name>
@cli help: Enable a built-in/plugin application codec
@cli help: (first built-in: monero-rpc; also requires
@cli help: per-key allow_codecs).

Allow an application codec id or alias (for example **monero-rpc**). Repeat for
multiple codecs.
@opt **--allow-monero-rpc**
@cli spell: --allow-monero-rpc
@cli help: Alias for --codec-allow monero-rpc

Convenience spelling for **--codec-allow** *monero-rpc***.**
@opt **--service-allow** *name*
@cli spell: --service-allow <name>
@cli help: Enable a native ABI named service stream
@cli help: (also requires per-key allow_services
@cli help: and yume_endpoint_register_service).

Allow a native ABI named service stream. The connected key must also list the
service in **permissions.allow_services** and the embedding process must call
man:yume_server_register_service(3).
@opt **--monero-rpc-backend** *host:port*
@cli spell: --monero-rpc-backend <addr:port>
@cli help: Loopback monerod RPC backend for the codec
@cli help: (default 127.0.0.1:18089).

Loopback backend for Monero RPC reconstruction. Default: **127.0.0.1:18089**.
Only loopback literals are accepted.
@end

## DISGUISE OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--hide-in-the-crowd** *profile*
@cli spell: --hide-in-the-crowd <p>
@cli help: HTTP-layer disguise profile for the disguise
@cli help: responses this daemon emits when probed.
@cli help: Values: nginx, nginx-stable, apache, caddy,
@cli help: cloudflare, express, gunicorn, none, yumed
@cli help: (default: yumed; nginx under --public-node).

Serve synthetic HTTP responses for non-YUME HTTPS probes using a named disguise
profile. Templates use common CDN or static-site error-page shapes but are not
native server implementations.
@end

## HOST CONTROLLER

@options
@opt **--host-mode** *mode*
@cli spell: --host-mode <mode>
@cli help: Host controller mode: off, private, relay.
@cli help: private = WAN host without YUME clients by default.
@cli help: relay = host routing plus YUME client tunnel.

WAN host controller mode: **off**, **private**, or **relay**. **private**
serves routed backends and rejects YUME clients by default. **relay** adds YUME
client tunnel support on the same listener.
@opt **--accept-yume-clients**
@cli spell: --accept-yume-clients
@cli help: Accept authenticated YUME clients (default).

Accept authenticated YUME clients (default when host mode is off or relay).
@opt **--no-yume-clients**
@cli spell: --no-yume-clients
@cli help: Reject YUME AUTH/carrier; disguise only.

Reject YUME AUTH and carrier paths; probes receive disguise responses only.
@opt **--client-deny-action** *action*
@cli spell: --client-deny-action <a>
@cli help: IP filter deny action: close, reset, drop.

How client IP filter denials close sockets: **close**, **reset**, or **drop**.
@opt **--exposure-check** *hostname*
@cli spell: --exposure-check <host>
@cli help: Probe whether hostname is direct TCP or CF HTTP.

Probe whether the hostname is reachable via direct TCP or sits behind a
Cloudflare HTTP proxy. Result is logged at startup and exposed in
**runtime.info**.
@opt **host_mode**

**accept_yume_clients** **client_deny_action** **routes** **listeners**
**exposure_check_hostname** Host-controller fields in **yumed.json**. Route and
listener backends currently support loopback TCP URIs only
(*loopback://127.0.0.1:PORT* or *loopback://[::1]:PORT*). See
**HOST_CONTROLLER.md**.
@end

## FEDERATION AND CLUSTER OPTIONS

Federation links are TLS 1.3 connections with server-certificate and hostname
verification by the dialing man:yumed-v2-reference(8). After H2-carrier admission, the
dialing node authenticates with exporter-bound AUTH v2 using the pairwise PSK
and its enrolled composite identity. The remote server identity comes from its
verified TLS certificate and hostname, plus an optional certificate pin.
Federation does not use TLS client certificates and is not mutual TLS. Each
peer keeps its own **authorized_keys** and **auth_keys.meta**.

@options
@opt **--cluster-bootstrap**
@cli spell: --cluster-bootstrap
@cli help: Mark this node as a cluster entry point;
@cli help: federation enabled but no outbound --peer required
@cli help: (other servers will dial in via --cluster-join)

Mark this node as a cluster bootstrap that accepts incoming peer dials. Implies
**--federation-enable**.
@opt **--cluster-join** *spec*
@cli spell: --cluster-join <spec>
@cli help: Join cluster via short form; implies --federation-enable.
@cli help: spec: [id@]host[:port]?psk_file=<path>&
@cli help:       carrier_secret_file=<path>[&pin=<sha256>]
@cli help: pin is 64 lowercase hex; IPv6 requires id@[host]
@cli help: repeat for multiple peers

Dial a peer using the short form
**[id@]host[:port]?psk_file=path&carrier_secret_file=path[&pin=sha256]***.*
Bracket IPv6 addresses as **[2001:db8::1]:443**. IPv6 joins require an explicit
peer-id prefix, for example **peer-v6@[2001:db8::1]:443**. When supplied,
**pin** is exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters (the SHA-256 of the TLS
leaf DER). Both secret-file parameters are mandatory. Repeat for multiple
peers.
@opt **--public-node**
@cli spell: --public-node
@cli help: Hardening preset for an internet-facing reference daemon.
@cli help: Rejects --allow-exec / --allow-local-ip /
@cli help: --control-full; requires --auth-keys and
@cli help: both protected YUME 2.0 secret files.
@cli help: Also implicitly sets --hide-in-the-crowd nginx
@cli help: when no profile is otherwise selected.

Internet-facing hardening preset. Rejects dangerous server flags, requires
**--auth-keys** and a nonempty **--obfs-secret-file**, a mandatory inner PSK
file, and the loopback Node backend, and tightens session limits.
@opt **--federation-identity** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --federation-identity <path>
@cli help: composite identity used for peer AUTH v2
@cli column: 31

Composite identity (Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 PEM bundle) presented when dialing
federation peers. The peer must enroll this identity in its **--auth-keys**
with a **federation_peer_id** and **federation_psk_file** meta entry. The
latter is the pairwise AUTH-v2 PSK for that exact identity; relative paths are
resolved from the metadata file. It is separate from the daemon's ordinary
client **inner_psk_file**.
@opt **--federation-operator-ca** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --federation-operator-ca <path>
@cli help: CA used to verify peer servers
@cli column: 34

CA bundle used by an outbound federation dialer to verify the remote **yumed-v2-reference**
server certificate and hostname. This option does not configure a TLS client
certificate; peer authorization occurs in exporter-bound AUTH v2.
@opt **--peer** *json*

Add a federation peer using raw JSON (power-user form). Each peer requires
**psk_file** (the pairwise AUTH v2 PSK, identical on both nodes) and
**carrier_secret_file** (the peer's admission secret, i.e. the value of its
**--obfs-secret-file**), because YUME 2.0 federation dials pass the same
H2-carrier admission as clients.

Federation is single-hop. A node advertises only its own local endpoints to a
peer, so every federated endpoint it knows is exactly one authenticated link
away and a federated relay channel never transits a third node. Federation does
not carry SOCKS, forward, reverse-forward, packet/TUN, or other exit traffic.
In a hub-and-spoke cluster two spokes therefore cannot reach each other; give
every pair that must communicate a direct peer entry. See
**docs/protocol/YUME_2_0_FEDERATION_TRANSIT.md** for the design that would
change this and the gates it must pass first.
@end

## FILTER ARCHIVES

@include shared/filter_archives.part

## CLUSTER INSPECTION

An attached **yumed-v2-reference** console accepts **help**, **status**, **sessions**,
**directory**, **peers**, **federation**, **topology**, **disconnect**
endpoint-id**,** stop**, and** quit**.**

@options
@opt **federation**

Per-peer configuration and link liveness. Peer rows report the dial address and
whether a TLS pin, pairwise PSK, and carrier secret were supplied; the secret
file paths themselves are never emitted on this surface. Overall readiness,
outbound-link readiness, and authenticated inbound-connection counts are
separate, so an inbound-only bootstrap connection is visible without claiming
that the node configured a dial-out link.
@opt **topology**

Draws the cluster: this node above its peers, with each peer's link state and
active channel count. Unparsed peer entries are reported as a count, because a
malformed entry has no usable id to name.

man:yume-net-map(1) prints the same drawing from a separate read-only binary,
and **yume-net-map --json** prints the underlying **federation.topology**
document. Both read the daemon's own graph, so a viewer never re-derives the
cluster from separate status calls.
@end

## AUTH_KEYS.META FORMAT

**auth_keys.meta** is JSON keyed by composite identity fingerprint (hex). Each
entry may include an **alias**, **key_type** ( **individual** or **bulk**),
decimal fair-share **weight**, optional **max_sessions**, and a **permissions**
object. Individual keys default to one session. Bulk keys use the server bulk
default unless a lower per-key cap is supplied, and they cannot receive
privileged controller, exec, LAN/full-control, codec/service, or federation
policy. Omitted dangerous permissions default to deny; relay-side
chat/file/bytes default to allow for individual keys and deny for bulk keys.

Example:

@code text
{
  "0d4f3a...fingerprint...": {
    "alias": "alice",
    "key_type": "individual",
    "weight": 1.5,
    "permissions": {
      "allow_exec": true,
      "allow_local_ip": true,
      "allow_codecs": ["monero-rpc"],
      "allow_services": ["example-service-v1"],
      "allow_chat": true,
      "allow_file": true,
      "allow_bytes": true
    }
  }
}
@end

Outbound administrator eligibility requires the visitor identity to be in the
physically separate **operator_keys** store and a different proved identity to
be in **admin_keys**. Visitor metadata cannot grant inbound admin, outbound
admin, or full control. A public key present in both regular and operator
stores is rejected.

Generate fingerprints with **yumed-v2-reference --auth-keys** *path* **--keys-list***.* See
**docs/PERMISSIONS.md** for the full field table.

## ENVIRONMENT

@options
@opt **YUME_DNS_SERVER**

Override the resolver used for server-side outbound opens when **--dns-server**
is not set.
@opt **YUME_ANONYM_LOCAL_SIGN**

When set to **0**, disable local operator identity proof signing even if
operator CA/delegated keys are configured. Set to **1** to force local signing
on platforms where it is off by default.
@opt **SUDO_UID**

**/** **SUDO_GID** When started under **sudo**, these are read after
bind/listen so PQ key files can be man:chown(2) ed before privilege drop.
@end

## KEY MANAGEMENT

@options
@opt **--keys-list**
@cli spell: --keys-list
@cli help: List authorized keys

List authorized keys.
@opt **--keys-add** *pub.pem*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --keys-add <pub.pem>
@cli help: Add authorized key (visitor store)

Add an authorized public key.
@opt **--keys-admin**
@cli spell: --keys-admin
@cli help: With --keys-add/--keys-gen-add: enrol into the
@cli help: separate admin store (--admin-keys). Admin still
@cli help: requires a distinct visitor key as first factor.
@cli continuation: 27

With **--keys-add** or **--keys-gen-add**, enrol the public identity into the
separate **--admin-keys** second-factor store instead of the regular visitor
store. An admin session still requires a distinct regular visitor identity as
its first factor.
@opt **--keys-remove** *id*
@cli spell: --keys-remove <id>
@cli help: Remove by fingerprint or alias

Remove an authorized key by fingerprint or alias.
@opt **--keys-alias** *id* *alias*
@cli spell: --keys-alias <id> <a>
@cli help: Set alias

Set or update a key alias.
@opt **--keys-gen** *prefix*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --keys-gen <prefix>
@cli help: Generate composite Ed25519+ML-DSA-87 identity (<prefix>.key/.pub)

Generate a composite Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 identity as *prefix*.key and
*prefix*.pub*.*
@opt **--keys-gen-add**
@cli spell: --keys-gen-add
@cli help: Append generated public key to auth_keys

Append the generated public key to **auth_keys**.
@opt **--ui**
@cli spell: --ui
@cli help: Interactive server manager

Open the interactive server manager.
@end

## AUTHORIZATION MODEL

Dangerous server features are behind three gates:

1. compile-time feature switches, such as **YUME_FEATURE_LAN_BRIDGE**;
2. runtime server flags, such as **--allow-local-ip**;
3. per-key metadata in **auth_keys.meta**.

A key without metadata can authenticate, but dangerous permissions default to
deny.

EXEC policy fields remain reserved, but this version has no command-execution
backend. Direct and relayed EXEC requests fail closed regardless of build flag,
server config, or key metadata.

## EXAMPLES

Start a basic server:

@code text
sudo yumed-v2-reference --listen 443 --cert /etc/yume/server.crt \
    --key /etc/yume/server.key --auth-keys /etc/yume/authorized_keys \
    --obfs-secret-file /etc/yume/obfs.hex \
    --inner-psk-file /etc/yume/inner.hex \
    --real-backend loopback://127.0.0.1:3000
@end

Start the mandatory 2.0 carrier with the real loopback Node cover:

@code text
sudo yumed-v2-reference --listen 443 --cert server.crt --key server.key \
    --auth-keys authorized_keys \
    --obfs-secret-file /etc/yume/obfs.hex \
    --inner-psk-file /etc/yume/inner.hex \
    --real-backend loopback://127.0.0.1:3000
@end

Block VPN-provider egress destinations using an operator-supplied archive:

@code text
sudo yumed-v2-reference --listen 443 --cert server.crt --key server.key \
    --auth-keys authorized_keys \
    --filter-list egress:deny:/etc/yume/vpn_db.tar.xz \
    --filter-geolite /etc/yume/GeoLiteCountry.tar.xz
@end

Generate and add a client key:

@code text
yumed-v2-reference --auth-keys /etc/yume/authorized_keys --keys-gen /tmp/alice \
    --keys-gen-add
@end

## FILES

@options
@opt */etc/yume/authorized_keys*

Authorized client public keys.
@opt */etc/yume/auth_keys.meta*

Regular-key permissions, type, fair weight, and session cap metadata.
@opt */etc/yume/operator_keys*

Physically separate operator/controller public keys.
@opt */etc/yume/operator_keys.meta*

Explicit operator policy metadata.
@opt */etc/yume/vpn_db.tar.xz*

Optional private VPN-provider blocklist archive. This file is operator-supplied
and should not be committed to the YUME source tree.
@opt */etc/yume/*

Common system configuration directory.
@opt *config/yumed.json*

Example local server configuration.
@opt */usr/share/doc/yume/*

Installed documentation in packaged builds.
@end

## REFERENCE BUILD

Enable **YUME_BUILD_TRANSPORT_V2=ON** in a separate CMake build to build
**yumed-v2-reference**. The reference binaries and these manuals remain in
the build and source trees; the default installation provides native YTP/1
**yume** and **yumed** instead. See **CONTRIBUTING.md** for dependency setup.

Read this manual from the source tree with:

@code bash
man -l docs/man/yumed-v2-reference.8
@end

